-- title:
--   The Massey ramp scheme of $C^+$ generated by $G^+=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\ v&G\end{bmatrix}$
-- statement:
--   For $G\in\mathbb F_q^{n\times k}$ and $v\in\mathbb F_q^n$ let
--   $$
--   G^+=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\ v&G\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb F_q^{(n+1)\times(k+1)},
--   $$
--   and let $C^+\subseteq\mathbb F_q^{n+1}$ be its column span. The **Massey secret sharing scheme** associated with $C^+$:
--
--   1. to share $s\in\mathbb F_q$, set $x_0=s$, draw $x_1,\dots,x_k$ uniformly from $\mathbb F_q$, compute $c^+=G^+x$, and give party $i\in[n]$ the share $c^+_i$;
--   2. given $T\subseteq[n]$ and shares $(S_i)_{i\in T}$, find the unique codeword $c^+\in C^+$ with $c^+_i=S_i$ for all $i\in T$ and output $c^+_0$.
--
--   This is the scheme of Theorem 6.14.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Coordinate $0$ of $\mathbb F_q^{n+1}$ is the secret's coordinate and party $i$ holds coordinate $i+1$ in `Fin (n+1)`. When no unique codeword agrees with the given shares the page's procedure is undefined; `Rec` then outputs $0$, so reconstruction can only succeed for every secret on sets $T$ where the codeword is unique.
-- source:
--   Doron, Leonov, Mosheiff, Navas, Resch, Ribeiro, Discrepancy for Random Linear Codes, arXiv:2606.24471 (PDF sha256 f03561c3…), §6.3, pp.47–48

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RLCDiscrepancy_Supplement_RampScheme

namespace RLCDiscrepancy.Supplement

/-- The **extended generator matrix** G⁺ = [1 0; v G] ∈ F^{(n+1)×(k+1)}_q (§6.3, p.47).
Doron, Leonov, Mosheiff, Navas, Resch, Ribeiro, *Discrepancy for Random Linear Codes*,
arXiv:2606.24471 (PDF sha256 f03561c3…), §6.3, p.47: "G⁺ = [[1, 0], [v, G]], where v ∈ F^n_q and G ∈ F^{n×k}_q."

**Formalization Note.** Rows and columns are indexed by `Fin (n+1)` and `Fin (k+1)`, index `0`
being the first row/column: row 0 is (1, 0, …, 0) and row `i.succ` is (v_i, G_{i,1}, …, G_{i,k}). -/
def gPlus {F : Type*} [Field F] {n k : ℕ} (G : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin k) F) (v : Fin n → F) :
    Matrix (Fin (n + 1)) (Fin (k + 1)) F :=
  Matrix.of (Fin.cons (Fin.cons (1 : F) (0 : Fin k → F))
    (fun i : Fin n => (Fin.cons (v i) (G i) : Fin (k + 1) → F)))

/-- The code C⁺ ⊆ F^{n+1}_q generated by G⁺ (its column span). -/
def cPlus {F : Type*} [Field F] {n k : ℕ} (G : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin k) F) (v : Fin n → F) :
    Submodule F (Fin (n + 1) → F) :=
  LinearMap.range (gPlus G v).mulVecLin

open Classical in
/-- The **Massey ramp secret sharing scheme associated with C⁺** (§6.3, pp.47–48).
Doron, Leonov, Mosheiff, Navas, Resch, Ribeiro, *Discrepancy for Random Linear Codes*,
arXiv:2606.24471 (PDF sha256 f03561c3…), §6.3, pp.47–48:
"• To share a secret s ∈ F_q, choose x ∈ F^{k+1}_q by setting x_0 = s and sampling x_1, …, x_k ∼ F_q.
Then, compute c⁺ = (c⁺_0, c⁺_1, …, c⁺_n) = G⁺x, and set the i-th share to be c⁺_i for i ∈ [n].
• Given a subset of parties T ⊆ [n] and the corresponding shares (S_i)_{i∈T}, find the unique
codeword c⁺ ∈ C⁺ such that c⁺_i = S_i for all i ∈ T and output s = c⁺_0."

**Formalization Note.** The coins are x_1, …, x_k, i.e. `Fin k → F` (uniform), and x = `Fin.cons s`
of them. Party i ∈ [n] holds coordinate `i.succ` of c⁺. Rec outputs coordinate 0 of the unique
codeword of C⁺ agreeing with the given shares on T; when no such codeword exists or it is not unique,
the page's procedure is undefined, and Rec outputs `0` (so reconstruction can only succeed for every
secret on sets T where the codeword is unique). -/
noncomputable def massey {F : Type*} [Field F] {n k : ℕ} (G : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin k) F)
    (v : Fin n → F) : RampScheme F n (Fin k → F) where
  share s x := fun i => Matrix.mulVec (gPlus G v) (Fin.cons s x : Fin (k + 1) → F) i.succ
  reconstruct T y :=
    if h : ∃! c : Fin (n + 1) → F, c ∈ cPlus G v ∧ ∀ i : T, c (i.1.succ) = y i
    then (Classical.choose h.exists) 0 else 0

end RLCDiscrepancy.Supplement


